<Base title="Electroporation & membrane biophysics" active="Research"><article><header class="container page-intro"><a class="back" href={sitePath("/research/#permeabilization")}>← Research</a><p class="eyebrow">Membrane biophysics · 2025</p><h1>Controlling the lifetime<br/><span>of a membrane pore.</span></h1><p class="lead">Using molecular dynamics to investigate how pulse modulation influences membrane permeabilization.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00249-025-01793-5" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the paper ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="#figures">Explore the figures ↓</a><a class="text-link" href={sitePath("/software/#diffusion")}>MATLAB transport code →</a></div></header><div class="container project-layout"><aside><p class="eyebrow">In this study</p><a href="#question">Research question</a><a href="#approach">Approach</a><a href="#findings">Findings</a><a href="#figures">Figure gallery</a><p class="aside-note">European Biophysics Journal<br/>54, 477–490 (2025)</p></aside><div class="article-body"><section id="question"><h2>Keeping a pathway open.</h2><p>Electroporation creates temporary pathways through a membrane. This study asks how the timing of electrical pulses can influence pore persistence and molecular transport.</p></section><section id="approach"><h2>Looking at the membrane directly.</h2><p>Molecular dynamics connects membrane structure with pore evolution. Simulation snapshots and quantitative profiles provide complementary views of the same process.</p><Figure src="electroporation-figure-3.webp" alt="Potential and density profiles alongside membrane snapshots during pore formation" caption="Figure 3. Profiles and molecular snapshots during pore formation."/></section><section id="findings"><p class="eyebrow">Central finding</p><h2>Pulse timing matters.</h2><p>The published simulations report that reapplying an electric field during the hydrophilic pore state can extend pore lifetime. Changing the interval between pulses provides a way to influence pore size.</p><p>These are simulation findings that inform the physical understanding of membrane permeabilization.</p><Figure src="electroporation-figure-7.webp" alt="Pore diameter versus time for no secondary pulse and three pulse intervals" caption="Figure 7. Pore-diameter trajectories under different pulse intervals."/></section><section id="figures"><h2>A closer look at the evidence.</h2><p>Open a figure to inspect its details. Original scientific colors are preserved.</p><div class="figure-grid"><Figure compact src="electroporation-figure-1.webp" alt="Simulation system, molecular pore detail and pore geometry schematic" caption="Figure 1. System and pore geometry."/><Figure compact src="electroporation-figure-2.webp" alt="Four electric-field pulse sequences over time" caption="Figure 2. Applied electric-field sequences."/><Figure compact src="electroporation-figure-4.webp" alt="Top and side views of pore evolution at six time points" caption="Figure 4. Pore evolution in molecular snapshots."/><Figure compact src="electroporation-figure-5.webp" alt="Pore diameter versus time in the XZ and YZ planes" caption="Figure 5. Pore diameter in two planes."/><Figure compact src="electroporation-figure-6.webp" alt="Molecular transport over time and comparison with molecular radius" caption="Figure 6. Transport and molecular-size comparisons."/></div></section><div class="paper-callout"><p class="eyebrow">Associated publication</p><h3>Optimizing electroporation via pulse modulation: a molecular dynamics study</h3><p>Emon et al. · European Biophysics Journal · 2025</p><a class="text-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00249-025-01793-5">Read the published study ↗</a></div></div></div></article></Base>
